<p>On September 7, 2026, XTEINK put the <strong>X4 Classic</strong> on sale through its own website for <strong>$79</strong>. Also called the X4 V2, the Classic is the button-first, no-touchscreen, no-front-light sibling of the touchscreen <a href="/blog/crosspoint-1-6-x4-pro-support-2026/">X4 Pro</a> that <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/e-readers/xteink-x4-classic" rel="nofollow noopener">Good e-Reader covered the same day</a>. The Classic is intentionally a smaller, lighter, less-expensive device: a <strong>4.3-inch E Ink panel</strong> in an ultra-thin body that weighs <strong>about 68 grams</strong> — roughly the weight of a deck of cards.</p>

<p>For EPUB readers who already maintain a sideloaded library, the X4 Classic is a deliberately stripped-down reader: physical buttons, magnetic pogo-pin charging instead of USB-C, and a fixed-format list (EPUB, TXT, MOBI, PDF) that covers almost everything a converter workflow produces. The trade-offs are real — no front light, no touchscreen, no Android app layer — but at $79 the device is also the cheapest brand-new pocket E Ink reader with EPUB support that XTEINK has ever shipped.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-x4-classic">What the X4 Classic Is (and What It Isn't)</h2>

<p>The X4 Classic is a pocket reader, not a smartphone-shaped slab and not a 6-inch Kindle replacement. The 4.3-inch E Ink panel sits between a credit card and a small paperback in terms of usable reading area. The form factor is closer to a phone-size E Ink note card than to a typical 6-inch e-reader.</p>

<p>The design choices line up with that positioning:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>No touchscreen.</strong> All navigation is via physical buttons on the front and sides. There is no capacitive layer over the E Ink panel.</li>
<li><strong>No front light.</strong> There is no LED ring under the bezel. Reading in dim environments requires an external clip-on light.</li>
<li><strong>No Android.</strong> Unlike the <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/e-readers/xteink-s4-android-sideload" rel="nofollow noopener">XTEINK S4</a> (the company's phone-shaped sideload-friendly reader), the X4 Classic runs a fixed-function firmware.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic backplate.</strong> The rear cover accepts magnetic accessories, similar in concept to a Magsafe phone case.</li>
<li><strong>Magnetic pogo-pin charging.</strong> The device has no USB-C port. Charging and data transfer use a proprietary magnetic pogo-pin cable that snaps to the bottom edge.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are not compromises dragged forward from the original X4 — many are explicit downgrades from the X4 Pro. The point is to hit $79 while keeping the parts that matter for reading: a fast-enough E Ink refresh, enough storage for a real library, and a battery that lasts more than a long flight.</p>

<h2 id="specs">Specifications at a Glance</h2>

<p>Specs below are from <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/e-readers/xteink-x4-classic" rel="nofollow noopener">Good e-Reader's launch coverage</a> and XTEINK's own product page:</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Spec</th><th>XTEINK X4 Classic</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
