<p>Two specs that aren't in the table matter more than they look. First, the microSD slot means the 16 GB of internal storage is not a hard ceiling — a 128 GB or 256 GB card fits the entire library of most sideloaded readers, which keeps the device future-proof as libraries grow. Second, the 920 mAh battery sounds small compared to a Kindle Paperwhite (1,700 mAh) or a Kobo Libra Colour (2,050 mAh), but the X4 Classic also drives a much smaller 4.3-inch panel with no front-light LEDs and no touchscreen sensor. Battery life in practice is closer to a month of regular reading than to a week, per the same Good e-Reader coverage.</p>

<h2 id="why-esp32">Why the ESP32-S3 Processor Matters for an EPUB Reader</h2>

<p>The X4 Classic uses an ESP32-S3, the same chip family that powers the <a href="/blog/crosspoint-1-6-x4-pro-support-2026/">CrossPoint Reader firmware</a> we covered last week. The ESP32 line is not what you'd find in a Kindle or Kobo — those use much more powerful application processors because they need to render full Android-like stacks. The ESP32-S3 is a microcontroller: lower clock speed, a few hundred kilobytes of usable RAM, and tight power budgets.</p>

<p>That tradeoff is intentional. For an EPUB-only reader, the firmware does not need to render an app launcher, manage background sync, or juggle a touch sensor pipeline. It just needs to parse an EPUB (which is mostly HTML + CSS), lay it out for a 4.3-inch screen, and turn pages fast enough that you don't notice the refresh. The ESP32-S3 does that without burning battery on parts of the system the X4 Classic doesn't have.</p>

<p>The practical consequence is that the X4 Classic runs a much simpler firmware than a Kindle does. That firmware is what XTEINK ships by default — but because the hardware is the same ESP32-S3 family that CrossPoint supports, the door is open for community firmware projects to target the Classic in the future. Nothing is announced, but the precedent (X3 → X4 → X4 Pro CrossPoint support in 1.5.0 and 1.6.0) makes a future Classic port plausible.</p>

<h2 id="formats">Format Support: Why EPUB, MOBI, and PDF Are the Right Three</h2>

<p>The X4 Classic reads EPUB, TXT, MOBI, and PDF — and notably does <em>not</em> advertise AZW, AZW3, KFX, or KEPUB support. For a sideloaded-library reader, this list is exactly what matters.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>EPUB</strong> is the open standard and the output format of most converter workflows. If you generate EPUBs from Markdown, HTML, DOCX, or PDF sources with a tool like <a href="/">converter-epub.com</a>, every file you produce will open natively on the X4 Classic without conversion.</li>
<li><strong>PDF</strong> support matters for academic papers, scanned books, and government documents. The 4.3-inch panel is small for PDFs, but the device handles reflow where the source PDF allows it.</li>
<li><strong>MOBI</strong> is the older Kindle format. Most modern Kindle content is KFX or AZW3, which the X4 Classic does not support, but legacy MOBI files (including MOBI files generated by older Calibre workflows) open fine.</li>
<li><strong>TXT</strong> is plain text — useful for coding snippets, technical notes, and writing drafts.</li>
</ul>

<p>What the list omits is also worth noting. There is no KEPUB (Kobo's EPUB variant) and no AZW/AZW3/KFX (Amazon's current Kindle formats). That means Amazon-purchased Kindle books will not open on the X4 Classic without first being converted via Calibre's KFX-input plugin or via the official Kindle-for-Web export. If you rely on Kindle Unlimited or buy most of your books from the Kindle store, the X4 Classic is the wrong primary reader — it is a companion device for sideloaded content. If you maintain a sideloaded EPUB library, it is exactly the right device.</p>

<h2 id="x4-classic-vs-x4-pro">X4 Classic vs X4 Pro: What $20–$40 Actually Buys You</h2>

<p>The X4 Pro launched on Amazon in early September 2026 at a higher price than the X4 Classic. The headline differences, per <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/e-readers/xteink-x4-pro-is-now-available-on-amazon" rel="nofollow noopener">Good e-Reader's X4 Pro coverage</a> and the X4 Classic coverage:</p>

<table>
