</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Display</td><td>4.3-inch E Ink, no touchscreen, no front light</td><td>4.3-inch E Ink, touchscreen, front light, 220 PPI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>~68 grams</td><td>Heavier (front light + touch sensor add layers)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thickness</td><td>Ultra-thin (not specified)</td><td>5.98 mm (slightly thinner than original X4's 6.4 mm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery</td><td>920 mAh</td><td>1,100 mAh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage</td><td>16 GB + microSD</td><td>16 GB + microSD up to 256 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAM</td><td>Not published</td><td>8 MB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Page-turn input</td><td>Buttons + optional shake-to-turn</td><td>Buttons + touchscreen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Front light</td><td>None</td><td>Yes (the most anticipated upgrade over the original X4)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cable</td><td>Magnetic pogo-pin (proprietary)</td><td>Magnetic pogo-pin (proprietary, same idea)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Primary retailer</td><td>XTEINK website only</td><td>Amazon + XTEINK direct</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The trade is straightforward. The X4 Pro is the better all-day reader — the front light alone changes how usable the device is in bed, on a plane, or in a dim café. The touchscreen makes navigating a 1,000-book library less of a button-mashing exercise. The X4 Classic is the better <em>backup</em> reader — small enough to live in a jacket pocket, light enough to forget you're carrying it, and cheap enough that losing it on a trip is not a financial disaster.</p>

<p>Good e-Reader's take in their X4 Classic coverage is that the X4 Pro is the better buy for most people. That is a fair verdict for primary-reader use. For a secondary pocket reader that lives in a bag or a coat pocket and only gets pulled out for one-chapter sessions, the $79 X4 Classic is the more rational pick.</p>

<h2 id="shake-to-turn">Shake-to-Turn: Gimmick or Useful?</h2>

<p>The X4 Classic supports shake-to-turn pages by default. A physical gesture with the wrist advances the EPUB to the next page, which sounds gimmicky until you actually try it on a crowded bus or while holding a grocery bag. With the device in one hand, you can flick your wrist and turn a page without using your thumb for the button.</p>

<p>It is, however, easy to trigger by accident when you don't mean to — a brisk walk or an over-the-shoulder reach into a backpack can register as a shake. The setting is disable-able in the firmware, and Good e-Reader's coverage suggests most reviewers disable it within a few days. Treat it as a tool you can opt into, not a default behavior you have to live with.</p>

<h2 id="pogo-pin">The Magnetic Pogo-Pin Cable Trade-Off</h2>

<p>Like the X4 Pro, the X4 Classic charges via a magnetic pogo-pin cable rather than USB-C. The Verge noted in <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/976391/xteink-tiny-e-readers-magnetic-usb-cable" rel="nofollow noopener">a September 8, 2026 piece</a> that all of XTEINK's tiny readers now use a proprietary magnetic cable, which is convenient when you're already in the XTEINK ecosystem and annoying when you're not.</p>

<p>Practical consequences:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>You'll need a second cable if you own multiple XTEINK devices.</strong> The cables are inexpensive on XTEINK's site, but they're not interchangeable with the USB-C cables you already have for your phone and laptop.</li>
<li><strong>Charging is easy to attach one-handed.</strong> The magnet self-aligns, which is genuinely easier than fumbling for a USB-C port in the dark.</li>
<li><strong>Data transfer is also via the cable.</strong> You can sideload EPUBs over USB without Wi-Fi, which is faster and more reliable than an over-Wi-Fi transfer.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you already use a XTEINK X3, X4, or X4 Pro, your existing cable works with the X4 Classic. If this is your first XTEINK device, budget for at least one spare cable — they are easy to misplace.</p>

<h2 id="who-should-buy">Who Should Buy the X4 Classic</h2>

<p>The X4 Classic is a deliberately narrow device. It is not a Kindle replacement. It is not a Kobo alternative. It is a small, light, distraction-free pocket reader for people who already maintain an EPUB library and want a device that disappears into a coat pocket.</p>

<p>It is the right device if:</p>

<ul>
<li>You sideload most of your reading material from <a href="/">converter-epub.com</a>, Calibre, Standard Ebooks, Project Gutenberg, or another EPUB source.</li>
<li>You read in well-lit environments — at a desk, on a commute, or outdoors — and don't need a front light.</li>
<li>You want a primary reader that doesn't pull you into a store, notifications, or an app layer.</li>
<li>You want a cheap secondary reader for travel, the gym, or the kitchen without worrying about losing a $300 device.</li>
</ul>

<p>It is the wrong device if:</p>

<ul>
<li>You buy most of your books from the Kindle store and read them in the Kindle app or on a Kindle. The X4 Classic does not read KFX or AZW3.</li>
<li>You read in bed without a clip-on light. No front light is the single biggest functional limitation of the device.</li>
<li>You want to take handwritten notes, install third-party reading apps, or sync via KOReader. The X4 Classic runs fixed-function firmware only.</li>
<li>You want a 6-inch reading area. The 4.3-inch panel is small for PDFs and for novels with footnotes.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="practical-buying">What to Do Before You Buy</h2>

<p>Two practical checks before ordering:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Confirm your library is mostly EPUB.</strong> If more than 20% of your current reading is in Kindle (KFX/AZW3) or Kobo (KEPUB) format, plan a batch conversion through Calibre before committing to the X4 Classic. Otherwise you'll end up with a $79 device that ignores half your library.</li>
<li><strong>Order a spare pogo-pin cable at the same time.</strong> Magnetic cables are easy to misplace, and XTEINK is the only source. If the cable is back-ordered when you need it, your reader becomes a brick.</li>
</ol>

<p>Both of those are easy to address. They just have to be addressed before you place the order, not after.</p>

<h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line</h2>

<p>The XTEINK X4 Classic is a $79, 68-gram, 4.3-inch pocket reader that does EPUB, MOBI, PDF, and TXT — and not much else. It is the right reader for a sideloaded-library workflow and the wrong reader for someone whose primary library lives in the Kindle or Kobo stores. As a primary reader, the X4 Pro is the better buy for most people; as a pocketable backup that disappears into a coat, the X4 Classic is hard to argue with at this price.</p>

